From: mscharged-port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] Keep spot attenuation finite for an unset light direction
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: host rendering correctness (all backends). Apply after 0082.

Charged's LoadGameObjectLight builds every world and character light in a
GXLightObj on the stack and never calls GXInitLightDir. With GX_AF_SPOT and
GXInitLightAttnA(1, 0, 0) the direction cannot change the result, so the
direction registers carry whatever the stack held. When that leftover is
1e19 or more along the light, the shader squared the cosine to infinity,
0 * inf gave NaN, max(0, NaN) gave 0 and the light vanished. On macOS this
happened in most runs (the stack contents differ per run): the lit 3D scene
of a match came out about half as bright as on Vulkan and in Dolphin, and
anything that changed what was left on the stack (the lighting log, inline
FIFO parsing) made a run bright by chance.

Bound the cosine to 1e19 (its square stays finite) and treat NaN as 0, so
zero coefficients stay zero whatever order a shader compiler evaluates in.
Cosines below 1e19 give the same result as before.

diff --git a/lib/gx/shader.cpp b/lib/gx/shader.cpp
index e98c620..7c39f26 100644
--- a/lib/gx/shader.cpp
+++ b/lib/gx/shader.cpp
@@ -814,8 +814,13 @@ auto lighting_func(const ShaderConfig& config, const ColorChannelConfig& cc, u8
   if (cc.attnFn == GX_AF_NONE) {
     lightAttnFn = "attn = 1.0;"s;
   } else if (cc.attnFn == GX_AF_SPOT) {
+    // A light with cos_att (1, 0, 0) ignores its direction, and games leave
+    // it uninitialised (Charged's LoadGameObjectLight). Garbage of 1e19 or
+    // more squared to infinity, 0 * inf gave NaN and the light went dark.
+    // Bound the cosine so zero coefficients stay zero; NaN counts as 0.
     lightAttnFn = fmt::format(R"""(
-          var cosine = max(0.0, dot(ldir, light.dir));
+          var cosine = dot(ldir, light.dir);
+          cosine = select(0.0, min(cosine, 1.0e19), cosine > 0.0);
           var cos_attn = dot(light.cos_att, vec3f(1.0, cosine, cosine * cosine));
           var dist_attn = dot(light.dist_att, vec3f(1.0, dist, dist2));
           attn = max(0.0, cos_attn / dist_attn);)""");
